657. Robot Return to Origin
Problem

Given a string moves representing the movement of a robot on a 2D plane:

U → Move Up
D → Move Down
L → Move Left
R → Move Right

The robot starts at (0, 0). The goal is to determine whether the robot returns to its original position after performing all the moves.

Approach

I use two variables:

v → keeps track of vertical movement.
h → keeps track of horizontal movement.

For every character in the string:

U increases v by 1.
D decreases v by 1.
L increases h by 1.
R decreases h by 1.

After processing all moves, the robot returns to the origin only if both h and v are 0.

Example
Input: moves = "UDLR"

U → v = 1
D → v = 0
L → h = 1
R → h = 0

Final position = (0, 0)

Output: true
Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n) — each character is processed once.
Space Complexity: O(1) — only two integer variables are used.
Key Concept

This solution uses coordinate tracking. Instead of storing the robot's position after every move, we only maintain the net horizontal and vertical displacement.

Language

Java

class Solution {
    public boolean judgeCircle(String moves) {
        int h=0,v=0;
        for(int i=0;i<moves.length();i++){
            if(moves.charAt(i)=='U'){
                v++;
            }
            else if(moves.charAt(i)=='D'){
                v--;
            }
            else if(moves.charAt(i)=='L'){
                h++;
            }
            else{
                h--;
            }
        }
        if(h==0 && v==0){
            return true;
        }
        return false;


    }
}

